slug:'encinitas',name:'Encinitas',title:'Encinitas Remodeling: Coordinate the Whole Property',
intro:'For an Encinitas renovation that grows beyond the existing interior, talk to Engineering while the drawings are still flexible. The City specifically recommends early engineering consultation for extensive renovations and additions because right-of-way dedication, public improvements or other site requirements may affect the project.',
sections:[
['A larger room can affect more than the house','Consider a proposed kitchen extension together with the path to the front door, roof runoff and the side-yard construction route. Draw those relationships before treating the addition as a simple square-foot price. A plan that works indoors can still need revision once grading and access are considered.'],
['Use Encinitas’s bundled-permit guidance','The City’s Building Division describes bundled applications for projects involving several permit types. It also warns that exemption from a building permit does not necessarily eliminate Planning, Engineering or Fire requirements. Submit one clear description of the entire project and ask which scope should lead the application.',0],
['Keep revisions on the approved record','Encinitas directs applicants to obtain a revision when an issued design changes, with additional coordination for significant new floor area. For homeowners, the practical implication is to resolve window sizes, wall locations and equipment placement before ordering. A late change can affect both the construction sequence and the permit record.',0]
],checklist:['Concept plan showing the house and proposed site changes','A list of work outside the interior: grading, drainage, paving and new openings','Questions for Engineering before full design fees are committed','A dated selection list identifying decisions that could change approved drawings'],
question:'Can I assume an interior permit also covers the new patio work?',answer:'No. Describe both scopes to Encinitas staff. The City may coordinate reviews, but an approval for one part of the project is not evidence that a separate improvement has been reviewed.',
links:[['Whole-home project planning','whole-home-remodel-guide.html'],['Outdoor improvements','pools-outdoor-living.html']],sources:[['Encinitas Building Permits and Inspections','https://www.encinitasca.gov/government/departments/development-services/land-development-building/building']]
