slug:'national-city',name:'National City',title:'National City Remodeling: Coordinate Permits & Trades',
intro:'A kitchen or bathroom can involve several permit categories even though it is one room. National City explains the difference between building, electrical, plumbing and mechanical permits, and describes combination permits for projects involving multiple trades. Start with a complete work list so the application matches the remodel.',
sections:[
['Follow the work behind the finishes','For a kitchen, trace the sink, range and exhaust routes on the existing plan. Moving one appliance can create electrical, plumbing or mechanical work beyond its cabinet opening. National City’s combination-permit description is useful when asking Building how the connected scope should be documented.',0],
['Make shared services visible in the plan','If the property has more than one occupied unit or shared utility connections, identify which occupants lose water, power or access during construction. Do not assume a project inside one unit has no effect on another. Confirm shutoff locations and restoration responsibilities before demolition. This is a planning scenario, not a claim about a particular National City property.'],
['Match the contract to the permit responsibilities','List the trades in the estimate and identify who coordinates their inspections and closes the permit. A proposal that includes cabinets but leaves ventilation or circuit work undefined is difficult to compare with a complete renovation. National City’s permit categories provide a useful checklist for the discussion.',0]
],checklist:['A room-by-room list of electrical, plumbing and ventilation changes','Utility locations and any shared shutoffs','Required access through neighboring or occupied spaces','A named party responsible for inspection scheduling and closeout'],question:'Does a cabinet installation quote cover all the permitted kitchen work?',answer:'Only if the written scope says so. Review electrical, plumbing, ventilation and building work separately, then confirm with National City how those components belong in the permit application.',links:[['Comparing contractor proposals','contractor-estimate-comparison-guide.html'],['Kitchen remodeling','kitchen-remodeling.html']],sources:[['National City Building Permits','https://www.nationalcityca.gov/government/community-development/building/building-permits']]
